# Hosting A Web Server From Home

This is the simple direct-to-Ubuntu setup: Nginx faces the internet and `systemd` keeps your Python app running. Use Gunicorn for Flask/SAS and Uvicorn for FastAPI/KC. Do not run Docker or Caddy for these apps.

## 1. Prepare The Server

Use a dedicated Ubuntu 22.04 or 24.04 server that stays on. Update it, then install Nginx, Python tools, Certbot, and the firewall:

```bash
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
sudo apt install -y nginx python3-venv python3-pip certbot python3-certbot-nginx ufw
```

Use SSH keys. Once a second SSH session works with your key, disable root and password login in `/etc/ssh/sshd_config`:

```text
PermitRootLogin no
PasswordAuthentication no
```

Validate and restart SSH. Keep the current session open until the second session succeeds.

```bash
sudo sshd -t
sudo systemctl restart ssh
```

Allow only SSH, HTTP, and HTTPS through the server firewall:

```bash
sudo ufw allow OpenSSH
sudo ufw allow 80/tcp
sudo ufw allow 443/tcp
sudo ufw enable
sudo ufw status
```

## 2. Configure Router And DNS

Give the server a permanent LAN address through your router's DHCP reservation page, for example `192.168.1.50`.

Forward only these ports in the router:

```text
WAN 80  -> 192.168.1.50:80
WAN 443 -> 192.168.1.50:443
```

Point your domain's `A` record at your public IP:

```bash
curl https://api.ipify.org
```

Do not forward SSH, database, app, or admin ports. If port forwarding does not work from mobile data, ask the ISP whether the home connection uses carrier-grade NAT. A normal port-forwarded home server cannot work through carrier-grade NAT.

## 3. Put The App On The Server

Create one folder and one locked-down service account per app. Replace `my-app` with the app name.

```bash
sudo adduser --system --group --home /srv/my-app my-app
sudo mkdir -p /srv/my-app
sudo chown my-app:my-app /srv/my-app
sudo -u my-app git clone YOUR_REPOSITORY_URL /srv/my-app/code
sudo -u my-app python3 -m venv /srv/my-app/venv
sudo -u my-app /srv/my-app/venv/bin/pip install --upgrade pip
sudo -u my-app /srv/my-app/venv/bin/pip install -r /srv/my-app/code/requirements.txt
```

Create `/srv/my-app/.env` for secrets, then restrict it:

```bash
sudo -u my-app nano /srv/my-app/.env
sudo chmod 600 /srv/my-app/.env
```

Keep real keys, database passwords, and Stripe secrets only in this file, never in Git.

## 4. Add The App Service

Choose one service below. Replace the user, paths, and Python import path with the real app values.

### Flask Or WSGI: Gunicorn

Create `/etc/systemd/system/my-app.service`:

```ini
[Unit]
Description=My Flask app
After=network.target

[Service]
User=my-app
Group=www-data
WorkingDirectory=/srv/my-app/code
EnvironmentFile=/srv/my-app/.env
ExecStart=/srv/my-app/venv/bin/gunicorn --workers 2 --bind 127.0.0.1:8000 wsgi:app
Restart=always

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
```

For SAS, the app path is normally `wsgi:app`.

### FastAPI Or ASGI: Uvicorn

Create `/etc/systemd/system/my-app.service`:

```ini
[Unit]
Description=My FastAPI app
After=network.target

[Service]
User=my-app
Group=www-data
WorkingDirectory=/srv/my-app/code
EnvironmentFile=/srv/my-app/.env
ExecStart=/srv/my-app/venv/bin/uvicorn app.main:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000 --workers 2
Restart=always

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
```

For KC, confirm the exact FastAPI import path before starting it. The important part is that Uvicorn binds only to `127.0.0.1:8000`.

Start the selected service:

```bash
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable --now my-app
sudo systemctl status my-app
curl http://127.0.0.1:8000
```

## 5. Configure Nginx

Create `/etc/nginx/sites-available/example.com` and replace `example.com`:

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com www.example.com;

    client_max_body_size 25m;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    }
}
```

Enable it, remove the default site, and test the configuration:

```bash
sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/example.com /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/
sudo rm -f /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/default
sudo nginx -t
sudo systemctl reload nginx
```

## 6. Turn On HTTPS

Wait until `http://example.com` works from mobile data, then let Certbot configure Nginx and request the certificate:

```bash
sudo certbot --nginx -d example.com -d www.example.com
sudo certbot renew --dry-run
```

Certbot adds HTTPS and redirects HTTP to HTTPS. It also installs renewal automation. Confirm it with:

```bash
sudo systemctl status certbot.timer
```

## 7. Update And Check The App

When deploying an update:

```bash
sudo -u my-app git -C /srv/my-app/code pull
sudo -u my-app /srv/my-app/venv/bin/pip install -r /srv/my-app/code/requirements.txt
sudo systemctl restart my-app
sudo systemctl status my-app
```

Useful checks:

```bash
sudo journalctl -u my-app -f
sudo journalctl -u nginx -f
sudo nginx -t
sudo systemctl status nginx
sudo systemctl status my-app
```

Back up the database and uploaded files somewhere outside the house. Test a restore before depending on the backup.

